---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';

/**
 * Install, from the README: build, run, and finish in the browser. Shiki
 * highlights at build time through `astro:components`, so nothing is hydrated;
 * the copy buttons are a dozen lines of inline JavaScript with no dependency.
 */
const shiki = {
  themes: { light: 'github-light', dark: 'github-dark' },
  defaultColor: false as const,
};

const steps = [
  {
    n: '01',
    text: 'Build the binary. Go 1.27+ is required until the first binary release.',
    code: 'go build -o bin/restorelab ./cmd/restorelab',
  },
  {
    n: '02',
    text: 'Start it. Nothing has to be configured first.',
    code: 'bin/restorelab serve',
  },
];

const terminal = `# Connect your cluster. Same password handling, same service account.
bin/restorelab connect https://pve.example.com:8006 --storage local-zfs

# see what can be recovery-tested
bin/restorelab workloads list --backups

# run a drill on VM 101, from its latest backup
bin/restorelab recovery test 101`;
---

<section class="section" id="quick-start">
  <div class="wrap section__grid">
    <div class="section__aside">
      <span class="section__index">04</span>
      <span class="section__label">Quick start</span>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="section__title">Two commands, then a browser</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        One binary, no agent to install on the guests, no daemon to keep alive
        beside it. That is the whole of it.
      </p>

      <div class="steps">
        {
          steps.map((step) => (
            <div class="step">
              <div class="step__head">
                <span class="step__n">{step.n}</span>
                <p class="step__text">{step.text}</p>
              </div>
              <div class="codeblock">
                <Code code={step.code} lang="bash" wrap {...shiki} />
                <button
                  class="copy"
                  type="button"
                  data-copy={step.code}
                  aria-label={`Copy: ${step.code}`}
                >
                  Copy
                </button>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <p class="prose" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
        With nothing configured, <code class="mono">serve</code> starts anyway
        and prints an address carrying a one-time setup token:
      </p>

      <div class="term" style="margin-top: 0.9rem;">
        <div class="term__bar">
          <span class="term__prompt">$</span>
          <span>bin/restorelab serve</span>
        </div>
        <pre
          class="term__body"><span class="term__warn">!</span> RestoreLab is not configured yet.
  Open this address to set it up. The token is printed once, and used once:

      <span class="term__rto">http://127.0.0.1:8080/setup?token=rls_...</span></pre>
      </div>

      <p class="prose" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
        Open it and the browser asks for your cluster&rsquo;s address, an
        administrator&rsquo;s password, and the storage drills restore onto.
        RestoreLab uses that password once, in memory, to create its own
        least-privilege service account, then throws it away — only the
        resulting token is stored, sealed with a master key it generates for
        you. It offers to create the isolated bridge on the same screen.
      </p>
      <p class="prose">
        When it finishes, the server restarts itself and the page you are
        already on opens your session. You land on the dashboard, connected,
        without going back to the terminal.
      </p>

      <div class="subhead">
        <h3 class="subhead__title">The same thing from a terminal</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          Every capability stays on the command line — it is what automation
          drives.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="codeblock">
        <Code code={terminal} lang="bash" {...shiki} />
        <button
          class="copy"
          type="button"
          data-copy={terminal}
          aria-label="Copy the three terminal commands"
        >
          Copy
        </button>
      </div>

      <div class="notes">
        <div class="note">
          <h3 class="note__title">Look before touching</h3>
          <p>
            <code class="mono">connect --read-only</code> produces a token that
            cannot create or destroy anything, and is enough for discovery and <code
              class="mono">recovery test --dry-run</code>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="note">
          <h3 class="note__title">Building without the front end</h3>
          <p>
            A binary built without the front-end toolchain has no interface
            compiled in and says so instead of 404ing; <code class="mono"
              >make ui</code
            > is what compiles it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="linkrow">
        <a href="/start/quick-start/">Full quick start →</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<script is:inline>
  document.addEventListener('click', function (event) {
    var button = event.target.closest('.copy');
    if (!button || !navigator.clipboard) return;
    navigator.clipboard.writeText(button.getAttribute('data-copy')).then(
      function () {
        button.dataset.copied = 'true';
        button.textContent = 'Copied';
        setTimeout(function () {
          button.dataset.copied = 'false';
          button.textContent = 'Copy';
        }, 1600);
      },
      function () {
        button.textContent = 'Failed';
        setTimeout(function () {
          button.textContent = 'Copy';
        }, 1600);
      },
    );
  });
</script>
